When a 2D canvas is larger than the GPU’s largest texture, Ladybird
presents it from a bitmap in memory, rather than over an IOSurface with
a Metal texture. The other engines likewise draw such a canvas without
the GPU. Blink’s BaseRenderingContext2D::CreateSharedImagePool() makes
no GPU pool when either side is past max_texture_size. In Gecko, a size
past mMaxTextureSize fails DrawTargetWebgl::Init(), so the canvas’s
CanvasRenderingContext2D::EnsureTarget() then falls back from its
TryAcceleratedTarget() to TrySharedTarget() and TryBasicTarget(). And
WebKit’s ImageBufferIOSurfaceBackend::calculateSafeBackendSize()
refuses a size past IOSurface::maximumSize(), so ImageBuffer::create()
falls through to an unaccelerated ImageBufferPlatformBitmapBackend.

In Chrome 158, Firefox 151, and WebKit 26.5, run on the same page, a 2D
canvas 16385, 20000, or 40000 pixels wide keeps working, and reads back
what was drawn at its far edge.
